Percentage change uses the original value; percentage points subtract rates; one basis point is 0.01 percentage point.|Change = (new − old) ÷ old.|A rate moves from 5% to 6%: +1 percentage point, +100 basis points, +20% relatively.|Always identify the denominator.|A rate moves from 6% to 6.5%. Change in basis points?|50|0.5|500
Ratios compare quantities; weighted averages reflect unequal exposure; indices express movement against a base.|Weighted average = sum of weight × observation.|60% earning 10% and 40% earning 5% gives 8%.|A simple average would wrongly ignore position size.|If both weights become 50%, the average is…?|7.5%|8%|15%
Simple interest applies to principal; compound interest also earns on accumulated interest.|FV = P(1 + r)^n for annual compounding.|₹100 at 10% for two years grows to ₹121, versus ₹120 simple.|Match the rate and compounding interval.|Third-year compound interest on this balance is…?|₹12.10|₹10|₹21
Arithmetic returns average periods; geometric returns describe compounding; log returns add across time.|Geometric growth = product of (1 + return).|+50% then −50% turns ₹100 into ₹75 despite a zero arithmetic average.|Losses and gains of equal percentages do not cancel.|What gain restores ₹75 to ₹100?|33.33%|25%|50%
CAGR is a constant compound rate connecting starting and ending values.|CAGR = (end ÷ start)^(1/years) − 1.|₹100 becomes ₹121 over two years: CAGR is 10%.|Annualising a short volatile period does not make it repeatable.|₹100 to ₹144 in two years implies…?|20% CAGR|44% CAGR|22% CAGR
Exponents model repeated growth; logarithms solve for time or growth rates.|Years = ln(target ÷ start) ÷ ln(1 + rate).|Doubling at 10% takes about 7.27 years.|Linear growth approximations become poor over long horizons.|At a higher positive growth rate, doubling time…?|Falls|Rises|Always remains seven years
Conditional probability updates beliefs using evidence; Bayes combines likelihoods with base rates.|Posterior probability depends on both test accuracy and prior frequency.|Of 1,000 borrowers, 10 default; a test flags 9 defaults and 99 non-defaults: only 9/108 flags are defaults.|A high detection rate need not imply a high probability after a positive flag.|The probability among flags is approximately…?|8.3%|90%|99%
Expected value averages outcomes; variance measures squared dispersion; covariance measures joint movement.|Portfolio risk depends on both individual variance and covariance.|A fair payoff of ₹0 or ₹100 has mean ₹50 and standard deviation ₹50.|Expected value alone omits downside and liquidity constraints.|A certain ₹50 has the same mean but…?|Lower dispersion|The same variance|Higher variance
Correlation measures standardised linear association between two variables.|It ranges from −1 to +1 but does not establish causation.|Two assets rise together in calm markets yet fall together more strongly during crises.|Historical correlation can change and miss nonlinear dependence.|Low past correlation guarantees crisis diversification?|No|Yes|Only for equal weights
Distributions describe possible outcomes; skewness captures asymmetry and kurtosis tail shape.|Inspect loss probabilities and extreme outcomes beyond mean and variance.|A strategy gains ₹1 repeatedly but occasionally loses ₹50.|A smooth average can conceal severe left-tail risk.|Which additional check is essential?|Tail-loss frequency and severity|Average gain alone|Share price decimals
Sampling estimates populations; confidence intervals describe uncertainty in an estimation procedure; tests assess evidence against a null.|Precision depends on sample size, dependence, and design.|A 95% confidence method covers the fixed true parameter in 95% of repeated samples under its assumptions.|A p-value is not the probability that the hypothesis is true.|What does a small p-value indicate?|Data are unusual under the specified null|The alternative is certainly true|The effect must be large
Regression estimates conditional relationships; statistical significance concerns evidence; economic significance concerns magnitude.|Separate association, forecast usefulness, and causal identification.|A fee effect is statistically clear but only ₹1 per customer.|Omitted variables and selection can bias coefficients.|Is statistical significance enough for a major investment?|No: costs and effect size matter|Yes: any low p-value suffices|Yes: regression proves causality
Time series are ordered observations; stationarity stabilises statistical properties; autocorrelation links periods; volatility measures variability.|Inspect trends, structural breaks, and dependence before fitting models.|Revenue trends upward; a regression in raw levels can create spurious relationships.|Independent-observation assumptions may fail.|For persistent observations, naive standard errors may be…?|Misleading|Automatically exact|Unnecessary
Optimisation chooses the best feasible allocation; vectors and matrices organise exposures; derivatives measure marginal changes.|Define the objective, constraints, and units before solving.|Choose weights summing to one while minimising wᵀΣw.|An optimiser faithfully amplifies flawed inputs.|If unrestricted optimisation creates implausible positions, inspect…?|Assumptions and constraints|Only font formatting|Only the output's precision
Sensitivity changes an input; scenarios change coherent combinations; simulation samples many outcomes.|Distinguish deterministic stress from probability-based sampling.|A model tests price −10% alone, then price −10% with volume −20%.|Simulation precision does not establish input validity.|Which is a scenario rather than a single-input sensitivity?|Price and volume changing together|Price changing alone|A cell's colour changing
